<h2 id="size-stride-alignment">Size, Stride, Alignment</h2>

<p>The basics of the memory layout of Swift struct instances</p>
<blockquote>
  <p>March 12, 2018 ∙ Swift Internals ∙ By Greg Heo</p>
</blockquote>

<p>When working with Swift types in memory, there are three properties to consider: size, stride, and alignment.</p>

<h2 id="size">Size</h2>

<p>Let’s illustrate with two structs:</p>

<div class="language-swift highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><table class="rouge-table"><tbody><tr><td class="rouge-gutter gl"><pre class="lineno">1
2
3
4
5
6
7
8
</pre></td><td class="rouge-code"><pre><span class="kd">struct</span> <span class="kt">Year</span> <span class="p">{</span>
  <span class="k">let</span> <span class="nv">year</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="kt">Int</span>
<span class="p">}</span>

<span class="kd">struct</span> <span class="kt">YearWithMonth</span> <span class="p">{</span>
  <span class="k">let</span> <span class="nv">year</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="kt">Int</span>
  <span class="k">let</span> <span class="nv">month</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="kt">Int</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></code></pre></div></div>

<p>My intuition says an instance of YearWithMonth is bigger than an instance of Year — it takes up more space in memory. But we’re scientists; how do we verify intuition with hard data?</p>

<h2 id="memory-layout">Memory Layout</h2>

<p>We can use the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">MemoryLayout</code> type to inspect some properties of our types in memory.</p>

<p>To find the size of a struct, use the size property with a generic parameter:</p>

<div class="language-swift highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><table class="rouge-table"><tbody><tr><td class="rouge-gutter gl"><pre class="lineno">1
</pre></td><td class="rouge-code"><pre><span class="k">let</span> <span class="nv">size</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="kt">MemoryLayout</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="kt">Year</span><span class="o">&gt;.</span><span class="n">size</span>
</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></code></pre></div></div>

<p>If you have an instance of a type, use the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">size(ofValue:)</code> static function:</p>

<div class="language-swift highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><table class="rouge-table"><tbody><tr><td class="rouge-gutter gl"><pre class="lineno">1
2
</pre></td><td class="rouge-code"><pre><span class="k">let</span> <span class="nv">instance</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="kt">Year</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">year</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="mi">1984</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="k">let</span> <span class="nv">size</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="kt">MemoryLayout</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="nf">size</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">ofValue</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="n">instance</span><span class="p">)</span>
</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></code></pre></div></div>

<p>In both cases, the size is reported as <strong>8 bytes</strong>.</p>

<p>Unsurprisingly, the size of our struct <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">YearWithMonth</code> is <strong>16 bytes</strong>.</p>

